Assume `Box` is a class with a public writable `int Count` field.

```csharp
static void Adjust(Box item, ref Box replacement)
{
    item.Count = 7;
    item = new Box { Count = 12 };
    replacement = item;
}
```

The caller runs:

```csharp
Box first = new Box { Count = 2 };
Box second = first;
Adjust(first, ref second);
```

Explain why `first.Count` ends as `7`, while `second.Count` ends as `12`. Describe what each parameter copies or aliases and why the caller's variables end up referring to different objects.

Consider this method:

```csharp
static bool TryReserve(int available, out int reserved)
{
    if (available < 3)
    {
        reserved = 0;
        return false;
    }

    reserved = 3;
    return true;
}
```

The caller declares `int count;` without initializing it, then calls `TryReserve(1, out count)`.

Explain why passing the uninitialized variable is allowed, why `count` is assigned even though the method returns `false`, and why deleting `reserved = 0;` would cause a compile error.

A caller passes an initialized `List<int>` variable to this method using `in`:

```csharp
static void Empty(in List<int> items)
{
    items.Clear();
}
```

Explain why the caller sees an empty list even though the parameter is readonly. Why would replacing the body with `items = new List<int>();` be rejected?
